<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">San Diego</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">San Diego is a port city in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. In 1769 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portola-expedition">Portolá expedition</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/junipero-serra">Junípero Serra</a> founded a presidio and the first mission of Alta California there. The town passed to Mexico and in 1848 to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and its harbour made it a coveted Pacific outlet, including for the Mormons' proposed State of Deseret.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/san-diego.jpg" alt="San Diego" width="320" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dirk Hansen · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Jolla_Shores_view_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>San Diego is a city on the Pacific coast of Southern California, next to the Mexican border. Its bay is the surface expression of a north-south nested graben, and the Rose Canyon and Point Loma fault zones are part of the San Andreas Fault system. About 40 miles east of the bay are the Laguna Mountains. The city is known for a mild <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> climate, beaches and parks, and its long association with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-navy">United States Navy</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area is historically home to the Kumeyaay people, whose town on the site was called Kosa'aay, &quot;drying out place&quot;. The San Dieguito complex was established in the area at least 9,000 years ago, and the Kumeyaay may have developed from it or arrived around 1000 CE, a question on which scholars differ. The first European to visit was Juan Rodríguez Cabrillo, who claimed the bay for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> in 1542 and named it San Miguel. In November 1602 Sebastián Vizcaíno surveyed the harbour, Mission Bay and Point Loma, and named the area San Diego de Alcalá after Saint Didacus.</p><p>The Presidio and Mission San Diego de Alcalá, founded in 1769, formed the first European settlement in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, and the city has been called the Birthplace of California.</p><p>In 1821 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> won its independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and San Diego became part of the Mexican territory of Alta California. The fort on Presidio Hill was gradually abandoned and the town grew on the level land below it. The Mexican government secularized the mission in 1834 and granted most of its lands to former soldiers. The 432 residents of the town petitioned to form a pueblo, and Juan María Osuna was elected its first alcalde.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> was ceded to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1848 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican-American War</a> and admitted as a state in 1850. That year San Diego was made the seat of the new County of San Diego and incorporated as a city, with Joshua H. Bean, the last alcalde, as first mayor. Two years later it was bankrupt, and the legislature revoked its charter and placed it under a board of trustees until 1889.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>San Diego is the seat of San Diego County and the second most populous city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, with over 1.4 million residents. Its economy rests largely on military and defence activities, tourism, international trade, research and manufacturing. The San Ysidro Port of Entry connects it with Tijuana, and the city's present charter was adopted in 1931.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “San Diego” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>San Diego</b> County has the largest Chamorro population on the mainland.</blockquote><a href="/culture/chamorro-people">Chamorro people · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Franciscans under Junípero Serra founded a mission at <b>San Diego</b> that year, and Monterey, founded in 1770, later became the capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/alta-california">Alta California</a></li><li><blockquote>Deseret claimed a huge area covering most of present-day Utah and Nevada, much of Arizona and parts of six other states, with a corridor to the Pacific at <b>San Diego</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-deseret">State of Deseret</a></li></ul></section>
